---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro'
import { DebtChart } from '../../components/islands/DebtChart'
import { StructuralGap } from '../../components/islands/StructuralGap'
import { VotedAndNot } from '../../components/islands/VotedAndNot'
import { NetInterest } from '../../components/islands/NetInterest'
import { AttributionSplit } from '../../components/islands/AttributionSplit'
import { LawExplorer } from '../../components/islands/LawExplorer'
import { BudgetChart } from '../../components/islands/BudgetChart'
import { budget, debt, laws, partySplits, vintageOf } from '../../data'

// Sections built so far. Everything else still falls through to the
// "Not built yet." placeholder below.
const HAND_WRITTEN = new Set([
  'forty-trillion',
  'whole-budget',
  'structural-gap',
  'what-congress-votes-on',
  'net-interest',
  'the-laws',
  'passed-signed',
])

const sections = [
  { id: 'forty-trillion', label: 'The $40 trillion' },
  { id: 'who-holds-it', label: 'Who holds it' },
  { id: 'how-old', label: 'How old is the debt' },
  { id: 'whole-budget', label: 'The whole budget' },
  { id: 'structural-gap', label: 'The structural gap' },
  { id: 'what-congress-votes-on', label: 'What Congress votes on' },
  { id: 'net-interest', label: 'Net interest' },
  { id: 'the-laws', label: 'The laws' },
  { id: 'passed-signed', label: 'Who passed it, who signed it' },
  { id: 'where-money-comes-from', label: 'Where the money comes from' },
  { id: 'by-state', label: 'By state' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'What this cannot tell you' },
]
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Government | US federal finance"
  description="Federal taxation, spending, debt and the votes behind them, FY1962 to FY2025."
  sections={sections}
>
  <p class="kicker">Route 3 of 3</p>
  <h1>Government</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">
    What the federal government collects, what it spends, what it owes, and who voted for
    each of it.
  </p>
  <section id="forty-trillion">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 1</span></p>
    <h2>Ten years, twice the debt</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      The US crossed $40 trillion in August 2026. Ten fiscal years ago it was $19.6
      trillion. Measured against the economy, though, the same decade moved the debt from
      105% of GDP to 124%.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      Debt doubled in ten fiscal years, from $19.57T at the close of FY2016 to $40T in
      August 2026, a 2.04x rise.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Debt doubled in ten fiscal years, from $19.57 trillion at the close of FY2016 to $40 trillion in August 2026."
      title="Total public debt outstanding at fiscal year end, 1995 to 2026"
      source={debt._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(debt._meta)}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="gross debt in nominal $ trillions, or percent of GDP"
      note="The final point is a daily close, not a fiscal year-end value, and carries no share of GDP because FY2026 GDP is not final."
    >
      <DebtChart client:visible rows={debt.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      Doubling is not new. FY2005 to FY2015 ran $7.9 trillion to $18.2 trillion, a 2.3x
      rise, faster than this decade. What changed is the base. That decade added $10.2
      trillion. This one added $20.3 trillion, an average of $2.0 trillion a year.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      CBO projected in May 2023 that the debt would reach $40 trillion in 2028. It arrived
      about two years early.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="whole-budget">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 4</span></p>
    <h2>One axis, everything on it</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Above the line, outlays stacked by category with revenue drawn across. Below it, the
      deficit that gap produces. Colour marks who held all three levers.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      FY2025: $7.01 trillion out, $5.24 trillion in, $1.78 trillion short.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Federal outlays from fiscal 1962 to 2025 stacked into mandatory, discretionary and net interest as a share of GDP, with a revenue line across them and the deficit drawn below zero. A three-row strip along the top shows which party held the presidency, House and Senate each year. Shaded bands mark the 2008 to 2009 financial crisis and the 2020 to 2021 pandemic."
      title="Federal outlays, revenue and the deficit, fiscal 1962 to 2025"
      source={budget._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(budget._meta)}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="outlays, revenue and deficit in nominal $ trillions, real FY2025 $ trillions, or percent of GDP"
      note="Mandatory outlays are charted net of offsetting receipts, so the three stacked segments sum to total outlays. Party control is curated for FY1995 onward only; earlier years show no control strip."
    >
      <BudgetChart client:visible rows={budget.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      Outlays and the deficit are the same arithmetic seen twice. Splitting them across two
      panels lets each be read on its own scale and hides how small the deficit is next to the
      budget that produces it. Here the deficit bar is the distance between the revenue line and
      the top of the stack.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="structural-gap">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 5</span></p>
    <h2>Revenue barely moves. Spending does.</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Revenue has sat in a narrow band for three decades. Outlays have not. The distance
      between the two lines is the deficit, in every single year.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      Revenue averaged 17.2% of GDP across the 31 years. Outlays averaged 21.1%. A
      four-point gap, every year.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Revenue averaged 17.2% of GDP against outlays at 21.1% across FY1995-FY2025, a four-point gap that closed only in FY1998-FY2001."
      title="Federal revenue and outlays by fiscal year, FY1995 to FY2025"
      source={budget._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(budget._meta)}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="revenue and outlays in nominal $ trillions, real $ trillions FY2025, or percent of GDP"
    >
      <StructuralGap client:visible rows={budget.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      That gap shows up in years with no recession and no major tax bill, which is what
      makes it structural rather than circumstantial. The only four years it closed were
      1998 to 2001.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="what-congress-votes-on">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 6</span></p>
    <h2>The part they vote on is the part that shrank</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Discretionary spending is the only category set by an annual appropriation. It is
      also the only one smaller today, relative to GDP, than in 1995.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      Share of GDP, FY1995 to FY2025: mandatory 9.8% to 13.7%, discretionary 7.2% to
      6.2%, net interest 3.1% to 3.2%.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Share of GDP from FY1995 to FY2025: mandatory rose from 9.8% to 13.7%, discretionary fell from 7.2% to 6.2%, and net interest moved from 3.1% to 3.2%, dipping to 1.2% in FY2015 before nearly tripling."
      title="Mandatory, discretionary and net interest spending by fiscal year, FY1995 to FY2025"
      source={budget._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(budget._meta)}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="mandatory, discretionary and net interest spending in nominal $ trillions, real $ trillions FY2025, or percent of GDP"
      note="Mandatory is net of offsetting receipts, the basis used throughout this section."
    >
      <VotedAndNot client:visible rows={budget.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      In real terms mandatory spending — net of offsetting receipts, the basis used
      throughout this section — grew 194% over this span and discretionary grew 79%.
      Mandatory runs on permanent law and net interest is set by past borrowing; neither
      requires an annual vote.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      Net interest looks flat endpoint to endpoint, 3.1% to 3.2%. It is not. It fell to
      1.2% of GDP by 2015 and has nearly tripled since. The endpoints hide the
      trajectory.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="net-interest">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 7</span></p>
    <h2>Nobody appropriates the interest</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Net interest is the compounding cost of every prior year's borrowing. It passed $1
      trillion in FY2025 and now exceeds defence spending.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      $232 billion in FY1995, $970 billion in FY2025. Across the 31 years, $9.4
      trillion, which is 39% of all deficits.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Net interest rose from $232 billion in FY1995 to $970 billion in FY2025, totaling $9.4 trillion across the 31 years, 39% of all deficits."
      title="Net interest payments by fiscal year, FY1995 to FY2025"
      source={budget._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(budget._meta)}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="net interest in nominal $ trillions, or real $ trillions FY2025"
    >
      <NetInterest client:visible rows={budget.data} />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      Thirty-nine cents of every borrowed dollar went to servicing the borrowing that
      came before it. There is no vote on this, no appropriation, no debate.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      The FY2015 figure of $223 billion is a recent trough, not the series low. The
      actual low is FY2003 at $153 billion. See <a href="#how-old">how old the debt is</a>
      for why this curve is so steep.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="the-laws">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 8</span></p>
    <h2>The bill arrives long after the vote</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Twenty-three major deficit-moving laws since 1995, with what each was scored to cost, who
      voted for it, and when it was signed.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      Sixteen of the twenty-three passed with cross-party majorities. Cross-party laws total
      +$9.24T against +$7.51T for all seven party-line laws combined.
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Sixteen of the twenty-three major deficit-moving laws since 1995 passed with cross-party majorities, totalling $9.24 trillion against $7.51 trillion for the seven party-line laws."
      title="Major deficit-moving laws, 1997 to 2025, against the annual deficit"
      source={budget._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(budget._meta)}
      xUnit="Fiscal year"
      yUnit="deficit or surplus as percent of GDP; ten-year score in $ trillions at enactment"
      note={`The chart window is FY1995 to FY2025; the underlying deficit series runs from FY1962. Each law's marker sits at its date's position within its enactment fiscal year, not on the year's tick. The two 1997 laws (Balanced Budget Act, Taxpayer Relief Act) predate the ten-year scoring convention and carry no score, so they are excluded from the cost totals. The CARES Act's House vote was a voice vote with no roll call. Three Senate votes (JGTRRA, the Inflation Reduction Act, and the One Big Beautiful Bill Act) tied 50-50 and passed on the Vice President's tiebreak, which does not appear in a roll call. Vote splits: ${partySplits._meta.source}`}
    >
      <LawExplorer
        client:load
        years={budget.data}
        laws={laws}
        splits={partySplits.data}
        threshold={partySplits._meta.cross_party_threshold as string}
      />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      A law is voted on once. Its cost runs for a decade or longer, under whoever happens to be in
      office later. The 2001 tax cuts were signed under unified Republican control, extended by a
      Democratic president in 2010, and made permanent in 2013 under a divided Congress. The 2017
      Tax Cuts and Jobs Act passed on a party-line vote, and most of its ten-year cost falls in
      FY2019 to FY2027, spanning a Democratic House and a Democratic president. The CARES Act
      passed with cross-party votes under a Republican president, a Democratic House and a
      Republican Senate.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      Scored laws net to $16.75 trillion against $24.15 trillion of deficits actually recorded over
      the same span. The gap is mandatory growth, demographics, revenue drift, recessions and
      compounding interest, none of which involves a roll call.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="passed-signed">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="section-no">Section 9</span></p>
    <h2>The same $16.75 trillion, two ways</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Every major law scored since 1995 nets to $16.75 trillion in ten-year costs. Sorted by
      the coalition that voted for it, and by the president who signed it. Both total the same
      number.
    </p>
    <p class="finding">
      By coalition: cross-party +$9.24T (16 laws), party-line Republican +$5.21T (3),
      party-line Democratic +$2.31T (4). By signing president: Trump I +$6.08T (5), Obama
      +$3.98T (6), Trump II +$3.40T (1), G.W. Bush +$2.79T (4), Biden +$0.50T (5).
    </p>

    <Figure
      ariaLabel="Two summary columns, each totalling $16.75 trillion in net ten-year legislative costs. The left column breaks the total down by the voting coalition that passed each law; the right column by the president who signed it."
      title="Net ten-year legislative cost by voting coalition and by signing president, laws enacted 1995 to 2025"
      source={budget._meta.source}
      vintage={vintageOf(budget._meta)}
      xUnit="$ trillions, ten-year score at enactment"
      yUnit="voting coalition, or signing president"
      note="Clinton's two 1997 laws are excluded: both predate the ten-year scoring convention and carry no comparable figure. The cross-party coalition passed 16 laws; 14 carry a score, and both counts are shown. Bars and totals are net figures, increases less reductions; gross increases alone total $20.73 trillion."
    >
      <AttributionSplit client:visible />
    </Figure>

    <p class="prose">
      These are net figures, increases less reductions. Gross increases alone come to $20.73
      trillion, and on that basis Obama reads $6.22T rather than $3.98T because he also signed
      $2.24 trillion of scored deficit reduction. If you show both, label both.
    </p>
    <p class="prose">
      A president signs what Congress sends. Coloring by control credits or blames one party
      for laws the other party largely voted for: the 2018 and 2019 budget acts passed under
      unified Republican control but cleared the Senate 71-28 and 67-28.
    </p>
  </section>

  {sections.filter((s) => !HAND_WRITTEN.has(s.id)).map((s) => (
    <section id={s.id}><h2>{s.label}</h2><p class="num">Not built yet.</p></section>
  ))}
</BaseLayout>
